description:
Edit repos/light-dispatch-vessel/src/index.ts to satisfy the following request. The relevant code is in the region "substrateGap_write". If that file is the wrong target, say so rather than editing it.

Close substrate gap orphaned-capability-scan-test-asserts-on-captured-reads-not-writes. The test harness captures every POST to /v2/impulses/resolve — reads as well as writes — so assertions that use emits[0] or emits.length are counting the resolver's substrateGap READ calls as if they were gap emissions. Five of six tests fail for this reason. The resolver is correct; do not change src/resolvers/orphaned-capability-scan.ts.

All edits are in test/resolvers/orphaned-capability-scan.test.ts. Each anchor below is unique — apply them one at a time, exactly as written.

EDIT 1 — replace this exact line:

    expect(emits[0].impulse.pointer.type).toBe("substrateGap_write");

with these two lines:

    const _w = emits.filter((e: any) => e.impulse.pointer.type === "substrateGap_write");
    expect(_w.length).toBe(1);

EDIT 2 — replace this exact line:

    expect(emits[0].impulse.pointer.gap.category).toBe("orphaned_capability");

with:

    expect(_w[0].impulse.pointer.gap.category).toBe("orphaned_capability");

EDIT 3 — replace this exact line:

    expect(emits[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id).toBe("orphaned-capability-problem_detection");

with:

    expect(_w[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id).toBe("orphaned-capability-problem_detection");

EDIT 4 — replace this exact line:

    expect(a[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id).toBe(b[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id);

with these three lines:

    const _wa = a.filter((e: any) => e.impulse.pointer.type === "substrateGap_write");
    const _wb = b.filter((e: any) => e.impulse.pointer.type === "substrateGap_write");
    expect(_wa[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id).toBe(_wb[0].impulse.pointer.gap.id);

EDIT 5 — the line `    expect(emits.length).toBe(0);` appears THREE times, in three different tests. It is not a unique anchor on its own, so locate each one by the line that precedes it and replace all three occurrences with:

    expect(emits.filter((e: any) => e.impulse.pointer.type === "substrateGap_write").length).toBe(0);

The three occurrences are preceded respectively by:
  (a) `      expect(body.gaps_emitted).toBe(0);` in the "does not emit for an invoked resolver" test
  (b) `    expect(body.gaps_emitted).toBe(0);` in the "classify-only mode (emit_gaps:false)" test
  (c) the degraded-guard assertion in the "does not storm when discovery/templates come back empty" test

Change nothing else. Leave wireSubstrate, TEMPLATES, the imports and every other assertion exactly as they are.

Why this is correct: the resolver already behaves correctly — body.gaps_emitted is 1 in the first test and that assertion already passes, so exactly one write occurs. The tests were measuring captured reads as emissions.

ORACLE — verified reachable, not assumed: I applied exactly these edits to a scratch copy and ran it. The suite goes from 1 pass / 5 fail to 6 pass / 0 fail with no change to the resolver.

The file must typecheck. This vessel carries a standing baseline of pre-existing failures elsewhere — judge on newly-failing tests, not absolute counts.

